## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming algorithms with bounded memory and error guarantees. Reservoir sampling is a fundamental technique that demonstrates trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and per-element computational cost.

## Key Concepts
- **Reservoir Sampling**: Maintains a uniform random sample of size S from an infinite stream
- **Unbiased Estimator**: (N/S) * sumS has expectation equal to the true sum
- **Relative Error Bound**: Using Chernoff bounds, P(|X - E[X]| > epsilon * E[X]) <= 2 * exp(-S * epsilon^2 / 3), giving S >= (3/epsilon^2) * ln(2/delta)
- **Time Complexity**: O(1) per element (constant-time random number generation and potential replacement)
- **Space Complexity**: O(S) for storing the reservoir

## Derivation
The probability that any given stream element ends up in the final reservoir is S/N. By linearity of expectation, E[sumS] = S/N * (sum of all N values), so E[(N/S) * sumS] = true sum — the estimator is unbiased. The variance of sumS scales as O(N * M^2 / S), and the relative standard deviation is O(sqrt(N/S)), giving the error bounds above.

## Code Example
```javascript
let S = 100;
let N = 0, sumS = 0;
let sample = [];

function process(value) {
  N++;
  if (sample.length < S) {
    sample.push(value);
    sumS += value;
  } else {
    let j = Math.floor(Math.random() * N);
    if (j < S) {
      sumS += value - sample[j];
      sample[j] = value;
    }
  }
}

function estimateSum() {
  return N === 0 ? 0 : (N / S) * sumS;
}
```

## Worked Example
M=100, B=10, S=50. Stream: [5, 12, 7, 23, 8, ...]. After first batch of 5 values (N=5): reservoir = [5,12,7,23,8], sumS = 55, estimate = (5/5)*55 = 55 (exact). After 100 values with sum=2340: reservoir has 50 values summing to ~1170, estimate = (100/50)*1170 = 2340 (close to true). With S=50 and epsilon=0.1, delta ≈ exp(-50*0.01/3) ≈ 0.85 — increase S to 400 for delta=0.01.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a stable online statistic with clear time/space guarantees using simple data structures.

## Key Concepts

- streaming algorithms
- two-heap median maintenance
- amortized vs worst-case costs
- space efficiency

## Code Example

```javascript
// production-ready sketch of two-heap median tracker
class BinaryHeap {
  constructor(compare){ this.data=[]; this.compare=compare; }
  size(){ return this.data.length; }
  top(){ return this.data[0]; }
  push(x){ this.data.push(x); this._siftUp(this.data.length-1); }
  pop(){ const top=this.data[0]; const end=this.data.pop(); if (this.data.length){ this.data[0]=end; this._siftDown(0); } return top; }
  _siftUp(i){ const x=this.data[i]; while(i>0){ const p=(i-1)>>1; if (this.compare(x, this.data[p])>=0) break; this.data[i]=this.data[p]; i=p; } this.data[i]=x; }
  _siftDown(i){ const n=this.data.length; const x=this.data[i]; while(true){ let l=2*i+1, r=l+1, smallest=i; if (l<n && this.compare(this.data[l], this.data[smallest])<0) smallest=l; if (r<n && this.compare(this.data[r], this.data[smallest])<0) smallest=r; if (smallest===i) break; this.data[i]=this.data[smallest]; i=smallest; } this.data[i]=x; }
}

class MedianTracker {
  constructor(){ this.lower=new BinaryHeap((a,b)=> b-a); this.upper=new BinaryHeap((a,b)=> a-b); }
  insert(x){ if (this.lower.size()===0 || x <= this.lower.top()) this.lower.push(x); else this.upper.push(x);
    if (this.lower.size() > this.upper.size()+1) this.upper.push(this.lower.pop());
    else if (this.upper.size() > this.lower.size()) this.lower.push(this.upper.pop());
  }
  median(){ if (this.lower.size() === this.upper.size()) return (this.lower.top() + this.upper.top())/2; return this.lower.top(); }
}
```

